[size=3][u][b]Player-facing UI[/b][/u][/size]

A [b]Hotkey Management[/b] entry is added to the top-level Options menu, right before [b]Settings[/b].

[b]Important[/b]: this menu is only available when the save is loaded or new game is started, i.e. is not accessible from the main menu!

[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/chemodun/X4-HotKeyAPI/refs/heads/main/docs/images/options_menu.png[/img]

It contains:

[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/chemodun/X4-HotKeyAPI/refs/heads/main/docs/images/hotkey_management.png[/img]
[list]
[*] [b]Hotkey Bindings[/b] - the actual keybinding page (native remap UI: click a row, press a key, confirm/replace on conflict). Conflicts are checked both against other hotkeys registered through this API and against vanilla bindings on other Controls pages.
[/list]
  [img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/chemodun/X4-HotKeyAPI/refs/heads/main/docs/images/hotkey_bindings.png[/img]

  [img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/chemodun/X4-HotKeyAPI/refs/heads/main/docs/images/warning.png[/img]

  [img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/chemodun/X4-HotKeyAPI/refs/heads/main/docs/images/hard_warning.png[/img]
[list]
[*] [b]Hotkey Requests[/b] - lists every id any mod has tried to register this session, regardless of whether it currently holds a slot, with a checkbox per row:
[/list]
  [img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/chemodun/X4-HotKeyAPI/refs/heads/main/docs/images/hotkey_requests.png[/img]
[list]
[list]
[*] [b]Checked[/b] (default) - enabled; holds a slot if one was free when it last registered.
[*] [b]Unchecked[/b] - blocked: the slot (if any) and its key binding are freed immediately, available for some other id to claim.
[*] Status column shows [b]Available[/b] (holds a slot, no key bound yet), [b]Assigned: \<key\>[/b] (holds a slot and a key), [b]Waiting (pool full)[/b] (enabled, but all 48 slots were taken last time it tried), or [b]Blocked[/b].
[*] Re-enabling a blocked id does not immediately reclaim a slot - press [b]Apply[/b] to re-run registration for everyone (this also re-checks for orphaned slots, see Clearance below).
[/list]
[*] A debug-logging toggle ([b]Enabled[/b]/[b]Disabled[/b]).
[/list]
[size=3][u][b]Selected/targeted object[/b][/u][/size]

Every hotkey fire attempts to resolve a "selected/targeted object" for the
currently detected area, [b]independent of `isObjectRequired`[/b]:
[list]
[*] [b]'map'[/b] - the map's current selection.
[*] [b]'pilot'[/b] - the piloted ship's current target.
[*] [b]'fps'[/b] - whatever the player's crosshair is currently over (soft-target).
[/list]
[b]isObjectRequired[/b] only controls whether the hotkey is [i]skipped[/i] when
nothing is selected/targeted - it does not gate whether the object itself is
passed once the hotkey does fire. So with [b]isObjectRequired = false[/b] (the
default), if something happens to be selected/targeted at the moment the key
is pressed, it is still passed to your callback; [b]isObjectRequired = true[/b]
only adds "skip firing entirely when there's nothing to pass" on top of the
exact same lookup.
[list]
[*] MD's [b]$actionCue[/b] receives it as `$object`, an MD component reference.
[*] Lua's [b]actionLua[/b] receives it as `object`, the raw component id (a real Lua
[/list]  value, not a blackboard round trip).

Both are absent/`nil` when nothing is selected/targeted for the detected
area.

Additionally, Lua's [b]actionLua[/b] receives in [b]fps[/b] mode the soft-target data as `softTarget`, a real Lua value (not a blackboard round trip), or [b]nil[/b] if there wasn't one.

[code]
  typedef struct {
  uint64_t softtargetID;
  const char* softtargetConnectionName;
  uint32_t messageID;
} SofttargetDetails2;
[/code]

[size=3][u][b]MD API[/b][/u][/size]

[size=3][u][i]Flow[/i][/u][/size]
[list=1]
[*] Your cue listens to  md.HotkeyApi.Reloaded
[*] Call md.HotkeyApi.Register_Action with your own callback cue
[*] Re-send Register_Action every time Reloaded fires again (cue references
[/list]   don't survive a Lua reload, so the registration itself doesn't either)
[list=1][*] When the player presses the assigned key, your callback cue is signalled
[/list]
[size=3][u][i]`Register_Action` - param fields[/i][/u][/size]
[list]
[*] [b]$id[/b] [i](string)[/i] - unique identifier of this action, chosen by your mod. The same id always maps to the same physical hotkey slot once assigned.
[*] [b]$version[/b] [i](number, optional, default `1`)[/i] - protocol version this request was built for. A version newer than this build supports is rejected outright rather than risk being silently misinterpreted.
[*] [b]$area[/b] [i](string, mandatory, no default)[/i] - one or more of `'map'`, `'pilot'`, `'fps'`, separated by [b];[/b] if more than one (e.g. `'map;pilot'`) - where the action is allowed to fire and what "selected object" means for `$isObjectRequired`. [b]'pilot'[/b] means the player is piloting a ship (no menu open). [b]'fps'[/b] means the player is on foot/first-person - not in any menu, and not piloting a ship in space (e.g. walking around a station or ship interior). Each area value has its own minimum supported [b]$version[/b] (currently `'map'`, `'pilot'`, and [b]'fps'[/b] are all available from version `1`) - a future area value added in a later version is only honored for a request that itself declares at least that version.
[*] [b]$isObjectRequired[/b] [i](bool, optional, default `false`)[/i] - if `true`, the action is skipped unless a map selection (`'map'`), ship target (`'pilot'`), or soft-target crosshair object (`'fps'`) is currently present.
[*] [b]$name[/b] [i](string)[/i] - display name shown for this action's row on the Hotkey Bindings/Hotkey Requests pages.
[*] [b]$actionCue[/b] [i](cue reference)[/i] - signalled when the hotkey fires and any target requirement is satisfied. Receives `param = table[$id = id]`, plus [b]$object[/b] (an MD component reference) if a selection/target was present - see [url=https://github.com/chemodun/X4-HotKeyAPI/raw/main/#selectedtargeted-object]Selected/targeted object[/url] above.
[/list]
[size=3][u][i]Debug logging (`GetDebugChance`) - recommended for cue-based consumers[/i][/u][/size]

A shared library, `include_actions ref="md.HotkeyApi.GetDebugChance"`, computes [b]$debugChance[/b] (`0` or `100`) from the player's current debug-logging preference (toggled from the Hotkey Requests page). Any cue - in this mod or a consumer's - can include it once and then use [b]chance="$debugChance"[/b] on its own [b]debug_text[/b] calls, so toggling the option silences everything in lockstep instead of each mod tracking its own flag. Cross-extension references must be fully qualified (`ref="md.HotkeyApi.GetDebugChance"`) - MD library references are namespaced per-script, not globally bare-addressable.

[size=3][u][i]Usage example MD[/i][/u][/size]

[code]
<cue name="Register_My_Action" instantiate="true">
  <conditions>
    <event_cue_signalled cue="md.HotkeyApi.Reloaded" />
  </conditions>
  <actions>
    <signal_cue_instantly
      cue="md.HotkeyApi.Register_Action"
      param="table[
        $id               = 'my_mod_my_action',
        $area             = 'map;pilot',
        $isObjectRequired = false,
        $name             = 'My Action',
        $actionCue        = My_Action_Cue,
        ]"/>
  </actions>
</cue>

<cue name="My_Action_Cue" instantiate="true" namespace="this">
  <conditions>
    <event_cue_signalled />
  </conditions>
  <actions>
    <debug_text text="'My action fired! param: %s'.[event.param]" chance="100" filter="general" />
  </actions>
</cue>
[/code]

[size=3][u][b]Lua API[/b][/u][/size]

[size=3][u][i]Flow (Lua)[/i][/u][/size]
[list=1]
[*] Listen for the "HotkeyApi.Register_Request" event (raised every time
[/list]   md.HotkeyApi.Reset_On_Lua_Reload fires - mirrors MD's Reloaded cue)
[list=1][*] Call HotkeyApi.RegisterAction({...}) in response, every time
[*] Your actionLua function is called directly when the assigned key is
[/list]   pressed - no MD/blackboard round trip at all

[size=3][u][i]`HotkeyApi.RegisterAction` - request fields[/i][/u][/size]

Same fields as MD's [b]Register_Action[/b] (using plain Lua values, no [b]$[/b] prefix), plus:
[list]
[*] [b]actionLua[/b] [i](function)[/i] - called as [b]actionLua({id, object, softTarget})[/b] when the hotkey fires and any target requirement is satisfied. [b]object[/b] is the selected/targeted component id (a real Lua value, not a blackboard round trip), or [b]nil[/b] if there wasn't one - see [url=https://github.com/chemodun/X4-HotKeyAPI/raw/main/#selectedtargeted-object]Selected/targeted object[/url] above. [b]softTarget[/b] is the soft target data (a real Lua value, not a blackboard round trip), or [b]nil[/b] if there wasn't one.
[/list]
Either or both of [b]actionCue[/b] and [b]actionLua[/b] may be set on the same registration; if both are set, only [b]actionLua[/b] fires (MD dispatch is skipped).

[size=3][u][i]Usage example Lua[/i][/u][/size]

[code]
RegisterEvent("HotkeyApi.Register_Request", function()
  HotkeyApi.RegisterAction({
    id = "my_mod_my_action",
    area = "map;pilot",
    isObjectRequired = false,
    name = "My Lua Action",
    actionLua = function(params)
      DebugError("My action fired! id: " .. tostring(params.id))
    end,
  })
end)
[/code]

[size=3][u][i]Registry inspection (Lua)[/i][/u][/size]

For consumers that keep their own copy of the registry somewhere the API cannot reach - most notably an isolated [b]ui/core/lua/*.xpl[/b] core script, which has no access to the registry, [b]ReadText[/b] or the blackboard.
[list]
[*] [b]HotkeyApi.GetActionNameByInputId(numericId)[/b] - the registered display name for an input-map id, or [b]nil[/b] if that id currently holds no hotkey. [b]numericId[/b] is what [b]GetCompassMenuMappings[/b] and the Controls page's [b]controlsorder[/b] rows carry (23-70), not the pool slot name.
[*] [b]HotkeyApi.IsDebugEnabled()[/b] - the state of the single Debug Logging toggle, so a consumer can gate its own logging on the same switch.
[*] [b]HotkeyApi.RegisterOnChanged(key, callback)[/b] - [b]callback[/b] takes no arguments and fires whenever a slot/id/name pairing or the debug flag changes; read the current state back through the two getters above. [b]key[/b] is the consumer's own mod id, and re-registering under the same key replaces the previous callback, so a Lua reload never leaves a dead closure behind.
[/list]
[size=3][u][b]Orphaned-slot reclaiming (Clearance)[/b][/u][/size]

Every slot loaded from a previous session starts each reload marked unconfirmed; registering again marks it confirmed. Ten seconds after every [b]Reloaded[/b] broadcast (long enough for every consumer to have responded), a [b]Clearance[/b] sweep clears the key binding and frees the slot of anything still unconfirmed - e.g. a mod that was removed, or stopped registering that id. This runs automatically; no action needed from consumers.

[size=3][u][b]Limitations[/b][/u][/size]
[list]
[*] [b]48-action hard ceiling.[/b] [b]INPUT_ACTION_*[/b] is a closed enum compiled into the game; this pool cannot be extended.
[*] [b]Cross-page conflict checking is area-aware.[/b] Remapping one of this pool's keys only checks for conflicts against the vanilla Controls pages that actually overlap the hotkey's own [b]area[/b] (and the same in reverse, when remapping a vanilla control that collides with one of this pool's keys) - not every page regardless of area.
[*] Loading a save without some of the mods that registered hotkeys will clear their slots and key bindings, so the player will have to reassign them when they reload with those mods again.
[*] The keybindings in X4 are not saved in the save file, but stored per "profile". Please note that the keybindings created for one game will be "replicated" to any other save using the same profile.
[/list]
